 #uuid "750758cb-b066-537b-acd0-366694517569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d26247f5-ea0b-58f7-ab8c-9e2873f56e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-170844" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\n- $P:{1,2,3}\\cap {4,5,6}=∅$\n\n- $Q:\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-{4}^{2}}$\n\n- $R:4$ is a prime number." :question/worked-solution "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options:\n\n- $P$ is a statement. This statement is *true* because the intersection of the given sets is empty.\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\n- $R$ is a statement. This statement is *false* since $4$ is not prime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35eba041-5109-5855-b132-174c0271c168" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8b6237d-6466-5901-b9f7-b72395141d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051688} {:answer/id #uuid "11a40b10-1e07-535b-a79f-7af083e205e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051689} {:answer/id #uuid "189a1623-f3db-54e8-b2b3-6c3e725fa1da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051690} {:answer/id #uuid "220562e4-1d0e-5fec-bc64-cbc3317d2eaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051691} {:answer/id #uuid "585c031c-74d4-510f-ab35-8cc0aa7184a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8b6237d-6466-5901-b9f7-b72395141d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051688} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeac440d-833d-5d59-a64c-fad9783ae979" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5327827-bdd3-59f2-8ac3-6f2fcb1af21e" :question/math-academy-id "q-171486" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n\n- $P(n):$ $n$ is even.\n- $Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 1$\n- $R(y):$ Draw a line of width $y\\,\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false. Rather, it will give an instruction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f185774-4d55-515c-9032-8f6832c6999a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Truth Value of a Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102b36c6-c1ab-59c5-9bce-d33570b0b7ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and 